# extraction — real Ruby source in, canonical expression text out.
#
# The impure edge hiding in the middle of the parser. A predicate is written as
# an ordinary Ruby block :
#
#   given("at most 10 toppings") { toppings.size < 10 }
#
# and the IR has to carry its TEXT, because a Proc is executable by Ruby and by
# nothing else. Recovering that text means asking the block where it was written
# and READING THAT FILE — disk IO, in the middle of what otherwise looks like a
# pure read of a declaration. An edge that leaves the domain is an adapter, so
# it is declared here rather than buried in a builder.
#
# Signal is REPLY : the parser hands over a block and gets a string back. There
# is no event, no verdict, no round-trip.
#
# Naming the port rather than the tool is what makes this swappable. `Prism` is
# one way to recover source — Ruby's own parser, which is why it is the one that
# ships — but a target that carries its predicates some other way (a sidecar
# file, a pre-extracted manifest, a different language's parser) implements this
# same port and the builders never learn the difference.
#
# It declares NO fields : where the source lives is a property of the block, not
# of a deployment, so there is nothing for a .world to supply.
Hecks.port "extraction" do
  verb   "extracted_by"   # the how-verb a bind hangs off
  signal :reply           # returns canonical text to the parser
end
